            {
              "id": "1736b4b2-27a1-3781-a71d-bf219e7c3cfb",
              "name": "Direct Writing to Response Object from User Input May Lead to XSS Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when data enters a web application through an untrusted source, in this case, the user input, and is used to write directly to a response object without proper validation or encoding. This vulnerability can allow an attacker to inject malicious scripts into web pages viewed by other users. These scripts can steal sensitive data from users, perform actions on their behalf, or even take over their accounts.\n\nIn the provided JavaScript code, the `res.send(author)` statement is vulnerable to XSS attacks because it directly writes the user input (`author`) to the response object without any sanitization or encoding.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always validate and sanitize user inputs. You should also encode the output before writing it to the response object. This will ensure that any malicious scripts injected by the attacker are not executed.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nYou can use the `escape-html` library to encode the user input before writing it to the response object. Here is how you can fix the vulnerability:\n\n```javascript\nvar escape = require('escape-html');\n\n// ...\n\nres.send(escape(author));\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `escape-html` library is required by the code example to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS (Cross Site Scripting) Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "7f366b51-0f8b-31b5-85f8-f2eb61e85fde",
              "name": "Direct Writing to Response Object from User Input May Lead to XSS Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when data enters a web application through an untrusted source, in this case, the user input, and is used to write directly to a response object without proper validation or encoding. This vulnerability can allow an attacker to inject malicious scripts into web pages viewed by other users. These scripts can steal sensitive data from users, perform actions on their behalf, or even take over their accounts.\n\nIn the provided JavaScript code, the `res.send(author)` statement is vulnerable to XSS attacks because it directly writes the user input (`author`) to the response object without any sanitization or encoding.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always validate and sanitize user inputs. You should also encode the output before writing it to the response object. This will ensure that any malicious scripts injected by the attacker are not executed.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nYou can use the `escape-html` library to encode the user input before writing it to the response object. Here is how you can fix the vulnerability:\n\n```javascript\nvar escape = require('escape-html');\n\n// ...\n\nres.send(escape(author));\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `escape-html` library is required by the code example to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS (Cross Site Scripting) Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "541ab5e1-7efc-39c7-b4e3-3f03c78d7961",
              "name": "Direct Writing to Response Object from User Input May Lead to XSS Vulnerability; Use 'resp.render()' for Safe HTML Escaping",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when an application includes untrusted data in a new web page without proper validation or escaping, or updates an existing web page with user-supplied data using a browser API that can create HTML or JavaScript. XSS allows attackers to execute scripts in the victim's browser which can hijack user sessions, deface web sites, or redirect the user to malicious sites.\n\nIn the context of JavaScript programming, directly writing to the response object from user input may lead to XSS vulnerability. This is because the `res.send()` function in Express.js sends the HTTP response and does not escape HTML characters. If the data passed to `res.send()` includes user input, it may include malicious scripts, leading to XSS attacks.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always escape user input that is included in a web page. This can be done by using context-specific escaping functions or by using a templating engine that automatically escapes user input.\n\nIn Express.js, you can use the `resp.render()` function to send the HTTP response. The `resp.render()` function renders a view and sends the rendered HTML string to the client. If you are using a templating engine like EJS or Pug, it will automatically escape user input.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of:\n\n```javascript\nres.send(book);\n```\n\nUse:\n\n```javascript\nres.render('book', { book: book });\n```\n\nIn this example, 'book' is the name of the view and `{ book: book }` is the data passed to the view. The view should be a file in the views directory and should use the syntax of the templating engine to include the data in the HTML.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- express: A web application framework for Node.js.\n- ejs or pug: A templating engine for Node.js.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS (Cross Site Scripting) Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Node.js Security Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Nodejs_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "50de0c4a-ce50-3a44-93b0-a9a17b26ca93",
              "name": "\"Potential XSS Vulnerability Detected through `ref` Usage\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when an application includes untrusted data in a new web page without proper validation or escaping, or updates an existing web page with user-supplied data using a browser API that can create HTML or JavaScript. XSS allows attackers to execute scripts in the victim's browser which can hijack user sessions, deface web sites, or redirect the user to malicious sites.\n\nIn the context of React, using `React.createRef()` can potentially lead to XSS vulnerabilities if the `ref` is improperly handled. This is because `ref` provides a way to access the properties of a DOM node directly, and if an attacker can control the value of `ref`, they can potentially inject malicious scripts.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should avoid using user-controlled input within your `ref` attributes. If this is not possible, you should properly sanitize and validate all user input to ensure it does not contain any malicious scripts.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `React.createRef()`, consider using callback refs. Callback refs give more fine-grain control over the values, which can help prevent potential XSS attacks.\n\n```javascript\n// Before\nnotificationSystem = React.createRef();\n\n// After\nnotificationSystem = (element) => {\n  this.notificationSystem = element;\n};\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `react`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS (Cross Site Scripting) Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP DOM based XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/DOM_based_XSS_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "480c84b3-1aea-33a9-83f3-ecc3f52dc9ec",
              "name": "Direct Writing to Response Object from User Input May Lead to XSS Vulnerability; Use 'resp.render()' for Safe HTML Escaping",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when an application includes untrusted data in a new web page without proper validation or escaping, or updates an existing web page with user-supplied data using a browser API that can create HTML or JavaScript. XSS allows attackers to execute scripts in the victim's browser which can hijack user sessions, deface web sites, or redirect the user to malicious sites.\n\nIn the context of JavaScript programming, directly writing to the response object from user input may lead to XSS vulnerability. This is because the `res.send()` function in Express.js sends the HTTP response and does not escape HTML characters. If the data passed to `res.send()` includes user input, it may include malicious scripts, leading to XSS attacks.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always escape user input that is included in a web page. This can be done by using context-specific escaping functions or by using a templating engine that automatically escapes user input.\n\nIn Express.js, you can use the `resp.render()` function to send the HTTP response. The `resp.render()` function renders a view and sends the rendered HTML string to the client. If you are using a templating engine like EJS or Pug, it will automatically escape user input.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of:\n\n```javascript\nres.send(book);\n```\n\nUse:\n\n```javascript\nres.render('book', { book: book });\n```\n\nIn this example, 'book' is the name of the view and `{ book: book }` is the data passed to the view. The view should be a file in the views directory and should use the syntax of the templating engine to include the data in the HTML.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- express: A web application framework for Node.js.\n- ejs or pug: A templating engine for Node.js.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS (Cross Site Scripting) Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Node.js Security Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Nodejs_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "48c9b2af-e11b-3130-858c-51ccd08969c4",
              "name": "\"Potential XSS Vulnerability Detected through `ref` Usage\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when an application includes untrusted data in a new web page without proper validation or escaping, or updates an existing web page with user-supplied data using a browser API that can create HTML or JavaScript. XSS allows attackers to execute scripts in the victim's browser which can hijack user sessions, deface web sites, or redirect the user to malicious sites.\n\nIn the context of ReactJS, using `ref` attribute can potentially lead to XSS vulnerabilities if the reference is used to directly manipulate the DOM with untrusted user input. In the provided code snippet:\n\n```jsx\n<NotificationSystem ref={this.notificationSystem} />\n```\n\nIf `this.notificationSystem` is influenced by user input, it could lead to a potential XSS vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate potential XSS vulnerabilities in ReactJS:\n\n1. Avoid using `ref` to directly manipulate the DOM with user input.\n2. Always sanitize user input, even if it is not directly inserted into the DOM.\n3. Use React's built-in protection against XSS. React escapes all variable content by default, so unless you're using `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` or manipulating the DOM with `ref`, you're protected.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nAssuming `this.notificationSystem` is a callback function that is used to store a reference to the DOM node, you should ensure that it does not use any user-supplied input to manipulate the DOM. A safe usage of `ref` would look like this:\n\n```jsx\n<NotificationSystem ref={notificationSystem => this.notificationSystem = notificationSystem} />\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `react`\n- `react-dom`\n- `react-notification-system` (assuming `NotificationSystem` is a component from this library)\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS (Cross Site Scripting) Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP DOM based XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/DOM_based_XSS_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "a308abbd-96f8-349a-b236-096a0a951a27",
              "name": "Critical Vulnerability in Babel Traverse Package, Fixed in Later Versions",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe Babel Traverse package version 7.22.5 has a critical vulnerability that could potentially be exploited by attackers. This vulnerability could allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code on the server where the application is hosted, leading to a serious security breach.\n\n## Security Concern\n\nThis vulnerability is a security concern because it could allow an attacker to gain unauthorized access to sensitive data, disrupt the operation of the application, or even take control of the server. This could lead to data loss, data theft, or a denial of service.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to update the Babel Traverse package to the latest version. This can be done by running the following command:\n\n```bash\nnpm update @babel/traverse\n```\n\nIf updating the package is not possible, you should consider using a different package that provides similar functionality but does not have this vulnerability.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn your `package.json` file, update the version of `@babel/traverse` to the latest version:\n\n```json\n\"@babel/traverse\": {\n  \"version\": \"latest\",\n  ...\n}\n```\n\nThen run `npm install` to install the updated package.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies to execute properly:\n\n- @babel/code-frame\n- @babel/generator\n- @babel/helper-environment-visitor\n- @babel/helper-function-name\n- @babel/helper-hoist-variables\n- @babel/helper-split-export-declaration\n- @babel/parser\n- @babel/types\n- debug\n- globals\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)](https://cwe.mitre.org/)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are valid and active for anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [],
                "severity": "HIGH",
                "kind": "FAIL"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "bec164a1-1bdf-3733-a505-ccfe571c5cd1",
              "name": "\"Avoid Spreading JSX Attributes to Prevent Unintentional Prop Passing and Invalid HTML Warnings\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn JavaScript, particularly when using libraries like React, developers often use the spread operator (`...`) to pass props (properties) to a component. This is a convenient way to pass multiple props at once, but it can also lead to unintentional prop passing and invalid HTML prop warnings. This happens when extra, unintended props are included in the object being spread, which can lead to unexpected behavior in the component or warnings from React about invalid HTML props.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this issue, it's recommended to explicitly pass only the props that a component needs, rather than using the spread operator to pass an entire object. This ensures that only the intended props are passed, and can help prevent unexpected behavior or warnings.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn the provided code example, the `Books` component is being passed all the props from the `Route` component, as well as an additional `user` prop. To fix this, you could explicitly pass only the props that the `Books` component needs. For example:\n\n```jsx\n<Route path=\"/books\" exact render={props => <Books match={props.match} location={props.location} history={props.history} user={this.state.user} />}/>\n```\n\nIn this example, only the `match`, `location`, and `history` props are passed to the `Books` component, along with the `user` prop. This ensures that no extra, unintended props are passed.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `react`\n- `react-router-dom`\n\n## OWASP and CWE Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top 10](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [CWE for Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)\n\nPlease note that while this issue can potentially lead to unexpected behavior in a React application, it's not directly related to a specific OWASP Top 10 or CWE vulnerability. However, it's always a good practice to follow secure coding principles and avoid potential issues like this one."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "b4517929-7da1-36fe-87b7-9bc6b65f6d74",
              "name": "\"Missing CSRF Middleware in Express Application\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF) is an attack that tricks the victim into submitting a malicious request. It uses the identity and privileges of the victim to perform an undesired function on their behalf. In an Express application, if CSRF middleware is not implemented, it leaves the application vulnerable to CSRF attacks.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should use CSRF protection middleware such as `csurf`. This middleware creates a CSRF token that is added to forms and AJAX requests. When the form is submitted or the AJAX request is made, `csurf` will ensure that the CSRF token is included and is correct.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nFirst, install the `csurf` middleware:\n\n```bash\nnpm install csurf\n```\n\nThen, use it in your Express application:\n\n```javascript\nconst express = require('express');\nconst cookieParser = require('cookie-parser');\nconst csrf = require('csurf');\n\nconst csrfProtection = csrf({ cookie: true });\n\nconst appDev = express();\nappDev.use(cookieParser());\n\nappDev.use(csrfProtection);\nappDev.use(function(req, res, next) {\n  res.locals._csrf = req.csrfToken();\n  next();\n});\n```\n\nIn this code, `cookie-parser` is used to read cookies, which is where the CSRF token will be stored. The `csrfProtection` middleware is applied to every route, and the CSRF token is added to `res.locals` so it can be accessed in views.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n- express\n- cookie-parser\n- csurf\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP CSRF Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross-Site_Request_Forgery_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-352: Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF)](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/352.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-352",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/352.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-352"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "979a782a-6de6-36e1-8af1-e1beefa50d27",
              "name": "\"Undetected CSRF Middleware in Your Express Application\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF) is a type of attack that occurs when a malicious web site, email, blog, instant message, or program causes a user's web browser to perform an unwanted action on a trusted site for which the user is currently authenticated. If the application does not have CSRF protection middleware, it is vulnerable to this type of attack.\n\nIn the context of Express.js, if the application does not use any CSRF protection middleware, it is vulnerable to CSRF attacks. This is because Express.js does not include CSRF protection in its core, and it must be added manually.\n\n## Mitigation\n\nTo mitigate CSRF attacks, you should use CSRF protection middleware. One of the most popular CSRF protection middleware for Express.js is `csurf`. This middleware creates a CSRF token that must be included in every state-changing request. If the CSRF token is not included or is incorrect, the request will be rejected.\n\n## Code Fix\n\nFirst, install the `csurf` middleware:\n\n```bash\nnpm install csurf\n```\n\nThen, use it in your Express.js application:\n\n```javascript\nconst express = require('express');\nconst cookieParser = require('cookie-parser');\nconst csrf = require('csurf');\n\nconst csrfProtection = csrf({ cookie: true });\n\nconst app = express();\napp.use(cookieParser());\n\napp.get('/form', csrfProtection, (req, res) => {\n  // pass the csrfToken to the view\n  res.render('send', { csrfToken: req.csrfToken() });\n});\n\napp.post('/process', csrfProtection, (req, res) => {\n  // process the request\n});\n```\n\nIn this example, the `csrfProtection` middleware is used in routes that render forms and process form submissions. The CSRF token is passed to the view when rendering forms, and it is checked when processing form submissions.\n\n## Dependencies\n\n- express\n- csurf\n- cookie-parser\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP CSRF Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross-Site_Request_Forgery_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-352: Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF)](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/352.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-352",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/352.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-352"
                ]
              }
