            {
              "id": "8adf429c-3d1b-39bb-895d-7d84e5d37424",
              "name": "\"Dynamic Input Validation for Client-Side Redirects to Prevent Open Redirect Attacks\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Dynamic Input Validation for Client-Side Redirects to Prevent Open Redirect Attacks\" is a vulnerability that occurs when an application accepts untrusted input that could cause the web application to redirect the request to a URL contained within untrusted input. By modifying untrusted URL input to a malicious site, an attacker may successfully launch a phishing scam and steal user credentials. Because the server name in the modified link is identical to the original site, phishing attempts may have a more trustworthy appearance.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to avoid using user input to determine the destination of server-side redirects. If this is not possible, ensure that the supplied value is valid, appropriate for the application, and authorized for the user. It is also recommended to use a list of authorized URLs defined in a configuration file and force any redirects to come from that list.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```javascript\nlet allowedUrls = ['/profile/users', '/another/path', '/yet/another/path'];\n\nwindow.location = state && allowedUrls.includes(state.from.pathname) ? state.from.pathname : '/profile/users';\n```\n\nIn the above code, we have defined a list of allowed URLs and we only redirect to `state.from.pathname` if it is included in the list of allowed URLs. Otherwise, we redirect to '/profile/users'.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet does not require any additional library dependencies to execute properly.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [Unvalidated Redirects and Forwards Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Unvalidated_Redirects_and_Forwards_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-601: URL Redirection to Untrusted Site ('Open Redirect')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/601.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-601",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/601.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-601"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "808c7564-10e9-3ed3-abb4-a9cdd1881ecd",
              "name": "Direct Writing to Response Object from User Input May Lead to XSS Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability \"Direct Writing to Response Object from User Input May Lead to XSS Vulnerability\" refers to a situation where user input is directly written to the response object without proper sanitization or encoding. This can lead to Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) attacks, where an attacker can inject malicious scripts into web pages viewed by other users. These scripts can steal sensitive information, perform actions on behalf of the user, or exploit other vulnerabilities in the application.\n\nIn the provided JavaScript code, the `_.pick` function from the lodash library is used to select specific properties from the `user` object and send them as a response. If any of these properties contain user input that has not been properly sanitized, this could lead to an XSS vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always sanitize and/or encode user input before including it in a response. This can be done using various libraries or built-in functions, depending on your specific technology stack.\n\nIn addition, you should also implement a Content Security Policy (CSP) to restrict the sources from which scripts can be loaded. This can help to prevent the execution of malicious scripts, even if they are included in the page.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nA simple fix for this vulnerability would be to sanitize the user input before including it in the response. This can be done using the `escape` function from the lodash library:\n\n```javascript\nconst _ = require('lodash');\n\nreturn res.send(_.mapValues(_.pick(user, ['name', 'email', 'role', 'username', 'website', '_id', 'credit']), _.escape));\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Express.js (for the `res.send` function)\n- lodash (for the `_.pick` and `_.escape` functions)\n\n## OWASP and CWE Resources\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "37142a78-c416-306a-9708-4e0d996bef96",
              "name": "Direct writing to Response object from user input may lead to XSS vulnerability; use 'resp.render()' for safe HTML escaping.",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) is a type of security vulnerability that enables attackers to inject malicious scripts into web pages viewed by other users. In JavaScript, a common way this can occur is when user input is directly written to the Response object without proper sanitization or escaping. This can lead to a variety of attacks, such as stealing user data, performing actions on behalf of the user, or even taking over the user's session.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, `res.send(user);`, the user input is directly sent to the response object without any form of sanitization or escaping. This means that if the user input contains malicious scripts, they will be executed when the response is rendered on the client side.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use the `resp.render()` function instead of `res.send()`. The `resp.render()` function automatically escapes HTML, which prevents any scripts from being executed. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is how you can fix the vulnerability in the provided code snippet:\n\n```javascript\n// Assuming Express.js is being used\nvar express = require('express');\nvar app = express();\n\napp.get('/', function(req, res){\n  var user = req.query.user;\n  res.render('index', { user: user });\n});\n```\n\nIn this code, `res.render('index', { user: user });` is used instead of `res.send(user);`. This ensures that the user input is properly escaped before it is sent to the client.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Express.js\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS (Cross Site Scripting) Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "5553a41c-448b-36b5-96b9-59a37472fbf8",
              "name": "\"Potential XSS Vulnerability Detected through `ref` Usage\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when an application includes untrusted data in a new web page without proper validation or escaping, or updates an existing web page with user-supplied data using a browser API that can create HTML or JavaScript. XSS allows attackers to execute scripts in the victim's browser which can hijack user sessions, deface web sites, or redirect the user to malicious sites.\n\nIn the context of React, using `React.createRef()` can potentially lead to XSS vulnerabilities if the `ref` is improperly handled. This is because `ref` provides a way to access the properties of a DOM node directly, and if an attacker can control the value of `ref`, they can potentially inject malicious scripts.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should avoid using user-controlled input within your `ref` attributes. If this is not possible, you should properly sanitize and validate all user input to ensure it does not contain any malicious scripts.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `React.createRef()`, consider using callback refs. Callback refs give more fine-grain control over the values, which can help prevent potential XSS attacks.\n\n```javascript\n// Before\nnotificationSystem = React.createRef();\n\n// After\nnotificationSystem = (element) => {\n  this.notificationSystem = element;\n};\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `react`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS (Cross Site Scripting) Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP DOM based XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/DOM_based_XSS_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "b478ca5d-f585-3d7d-9c25-b29583e61026",
              "name": "\"High Severity Vulnerability in Axios Package Version 1.4.0, Fixed in Version 1.7.4 (CVE-2024-39338)\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe Axios package version 1.4.0 has a high severity vulnerability (CVE-2024-39338). This vulnerability allows an attacker to perform a Server Side Request Forgery (SSRF) attack. SSRF is a dangerous vulnerability that can allow attackers to abuse functionality on the server to read or update internal resources.\n\n## Security Concern\n\nThe attacker can target internal systems behind firewalls that are normally inaccessible from the external network. They can trick the server into making a request that was not intended, leading to potential unauthorized access, data exposure, or denial of service.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nUpgrade the Axios package to version 1.7.4 or later to fix this vulnerability. Regularly update all your dependencies to their latest versions to protect against known vulnerabilities.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nUpdate the Axios package version in your `package.json` file:\n\n```json\n\"axios\": {\n  \"version\": \"1.7.4\",\n  \"resolved\": \"https://registry.npmjs.org/axios/-/axios-1.7.4.tgz\",\n  \"integrity\": \"sha512-...\",\n  \"requires\": {\n    \"follow-redirects\": \"^1.15.0\",\n    \"form-data\": \"^4.0.0\",\n    \"proxy-from-env\": \"^1.1.0\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThen run `npm install` to update your `node_modules` and `package-lock.json`.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies to execute properly:\n\n- follow-redirects version 1.15.0 or later\n- form-data version 4.0.0 or later\n- proxy-from-env version 1.1.0 or later\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Server Side Request Forgery](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Server_Side_Request_Forgery)\n- [Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE-918)](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/918.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-918",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/918.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "HIGH",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-918"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "d2a29cb0-2ef9-31ed-b161-dca57fb84f94",
              "name": "\"HTML Coding Risks XSS Attack Exposure\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe code snippet provided is vulnerable to Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) attacks. XSS is a type of injection security vulnerability where an attacker can inject malicious scripts into webpages viewed by other users. In this case, the `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` function in React is being used to directly insert user input (`this.state.result`) into the webpage's HTML. If `this.state.result` contains a malicious script, it will be executed when the page is loaded.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should avoid using `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` whenever possible. Instead, use React's built-in JSX syntax to set the content of elements. JSX automatically escapes all variable content, so you don't have to worry about XSS attacks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere's how you can fix the vulnerability in the provided code:\n\n```jsx\n<p className='lead'>Thank you {this.state.result} for subscribing!</p>\n```\n\nIn this fixed code, `this.state.result` is inserted as a JavaScript expression inside the JSX syntax. This means that it will be automatically escaped, preventing any scripts from being executed.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies to execute properly:\n\n- react\n\n## OWASP and CWE Resources\n\n- [OWASP XSS (Cross Site Scripting) Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ed836ef6-5b3a-3093-870f-c817cf9fa42b",
              "name": "Direct Writing to Response Object from User Input May Lead to XSS Vulnerability; Use 'resp.render()' for Safe HTML Escaping",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when an application includes untrusted data in a new web page without proper validation or escaping, or updates an existing web page with user-supplied data using a browser API that can create HTML or JavaScript. XSS allows attackers to execute scripts in the victim's browser which can hijack user sessions, deface web sites, or redirect the user to malicious sites.\n\nIn the context of JavaScript programming, directly writing to the response object from user input may lead to XSS vulnerability. This is because the `res.send()` function in Express.js sends the HTTP response and does not escape HTML characters. If the data passed to `res.send()` includes user input, it may include malicious scripts, leading to XSS attacks.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always escape user input that is included in a web page. This can be done by using context-specific escaping functions or by using a templating engine that automatically escapes user input.\n\nIn Express.js, you can use the `resp.render()` function to send the HTTP response. The `resp.render()` function renders a view and sends the rendered HTML string to the client. If you are using a templating engine like EJS or Pug, it will automatically escape user input.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of:\n\n```javascript\nres.send(book);\n```\n\nUse:\n\n```javascript\nres.render('book', { book: book });\n```\n\nIn this example, 'book' is the name of the view and `{ book: book }` is the data passed to the view. The view should be a file in the views directory and should use the syntax of the templating engine to include the data in the HTML.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- express: A web application framework for Node.js.\n- ejs or pug: A templating engine for Node.js.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS (Cross Site Scripting) Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Node.js Security Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Nodejs_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
