            {
              "id": "e8e4147f-0ebf-3baf-8e30-8e4749dd468e",
              "name": "\"Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nA potential Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerability can occur when user-controlled data is used in HTML strings. This can allow an attacker to inject malicious scripts into web pages viewed by other users. In the provided JavaScript code, the `author._id` is directly embedded into the HTML string which can be a potential source of XSS attacks if the `author._id` is user-controlled and not properly sanitized.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to properly sanitize and escape all user-controlled data before using it in HTML strings. This can be done by using built-in functions or libraries that can sanitize and escape special characters in the user-controlled data.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn the provided code, you can use the `encodeURIComponent` function to sanitize the `author._id` before using it in the HTML string:\n\n```javascript\ncontent: author => (\n  <Link to={`/authors/${encodeURIComponent(author._id)}`}>\n      <button\n      className=\"btn btn-primary btn-sm\">Update</button>\n  </Link>\n)\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- React\n- react-router-dom\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "e0a2030d-903d-3240-9b59-137f29fc85c5",
              "name": "\"Critical Vulnerability in form-data Package, Versions 3.0.1: Resolved in Versions 2.5.4, 3.0.4, 4.0.4 (CVE-2025-7783)\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `form-data` package versions 3.0.1 in Docker programming language has a critical vulnerability (CVE-2025-7783). This vulnerability is a security concern because it allows an attacker to exploit the system by injecting malicious code or manipulating the data being processed. This can lead to unauthorized access, data corruption, or even system crashes.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to upgrade the `form-data` package to versions 2.5.4, 3.0.4, or 4.0.4 where the vulnerability has been resolved. Regularly updating and patching your software is a good practice to prevent such vulnerabilities.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nTo fix this vulnerability, you need to update the `form-data` package in your `package.json` file:\n\n```json\n\"form-data\": {\n  \"version\": \"3.0.4\",\n  \"resolved\": \"https://registry.npmjs.org/form-data/-/form-data-3.0.4.tgz\",\n  \"integrity\": \"sha512-RHkBKtLWUVwd7SqRIvCZMEvAMoGUp0XU+seQiZejj0COz3RI3hWP4sCv3gZWWLjJTd7rGwcsF5eKZGii0r/hbg==\",\n  \"requires\": {\n    \"asynckit\": \"^0.4.0\",\n    \"combined-stream\": \"^1.0.8\",\n    \"mime-types\": \"^2.1.12\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThen run `npm install` to install the updated package.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies to execute properly:\n\n- `asynckit`: \"^0.4.0\"\n- `combined-stream`: \"^1.0.8\"\n- `mime-types`: \"^2.1.12\"\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten Security Risks](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)](https://cwe.mitre.org/index.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are valid and active for anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [],
                "severity": "HIGH",
                "kind": "FAIL"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "60f4b026-97cb-33c4-8b3d-c91a60600b10",
              "name": "Potential Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) Vulnerability Detected in 'href' Attribute of Anchor Tag",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) is a type of security vulnerability that enables attackers to inject malicious scripts into web pages viewed by other users. A type of XSS vulnerability can occur when an application includes untrusted data in a new web page without proper validation or escaping, or updates an existing web page with user-supplied data using a browser API that can create HTML or JavaScript.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the potential XSS vulnerability lies in the 'href' attribute of the anchor tag. If the `author._id` is not properly sanitized and is controlled by the user, it can lead to an XSS attack.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n1. **Escape User Input**: Always escape user input to make sure that any characters that have a special meaning in HTML are escaped to their HTML entities.\n\n2. **Use Safe APIs**: Some APIs provide automatic escaping. For example, using `textContent` instead of `innerHTML` prevents from XSS because it automatically escapes the input.\n\n3. **Content Security Policy (CSP)**: Implementing CSP can effectively reduce the risk of XSS attacks by declaring which dynamic resources are allowed to load.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn the provided code, you can use a library like `lodash` to escape the `author._id`:\n\n```javascript\nimport _ from 'lodash';\n\ncontent: author => (\n  <Link to={`/authors/${_.escape(author._id)}`}>\n      <button\n      className=\"btn btn-primary btn-sm\">Update</button>\n  </Link>\n)\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- react\n- react-router-dom\n- lodash\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "1231bad0-c035-34f8-867d-80f7d1306899",
              "name": "\"Potential XSS Vulnerability Detected through `ref` Usage\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when an application includes untrusted data in a new web page without proper validation or escaping, or updates an existing web page with user-supplied data using a browser API that can create HTML or JavaScript. XSS allows attackers to execute scripts in the victim's browser which can hijack user sessions, deface web sites, or redirect the user to malicious sites.\n\nIn the context of ReactJS, using `ref` attribute can potentially lead to XSS vulnerabilities if the reference is used to directly manipulate the DOM with untrusted user input. In the provided code snippet:\n\n```jsx\n<NotificationSystem ref={this.notificationSystem} />\n```\n\nIf `this.notificationSystem` is influenced by user input, it could lead to a potential XSS vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate potential XSS vulnerabilities in ReactJS:\n\n1. Avoid using `ref` to directly manipulate the DOM with user input.\n2. Always sanitize user input, even if it is not directly inserted into the DOM.\n3. Use React's built-in protection against XSS. React escapes all variable content by default, so unless you're using `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` or manipulating the DOM with `ref`, you're protected.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nAssuming `this.notificationSystem` is a callback function that is used to store a reference to the DOM node, you should ensure that it does not use any user-supplied input to manipulate the DOM. A safe usage of `ref` would look like this:\n\n```jsx\n<NotificationSystem ref={notificationSystem => this.notificationSystem = notificationSystem} />\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `react`\n- `react-dom`\n- `react-notification-system` (assuming `NotificationSystem` is a component from this library)\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS (Cross Site Scripting) Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP DOM based XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/DOM_based_XSS_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "7d5b9252-19c2-308f-baa5-a065f35c6b5e",
              "name": "\"High-Risk Vulnerability in HTTP Proxy Middleware Package, Resolved in Later Versions\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `http-proxy-middleware` package, specifically version `2.0.6`. This package is used in Docker programming to create a proxy middleware for HTTP. The vulnerability is considered high-risk, which means it can potentially lead to serious security breaches if exploited.\n\nThe security concern arises from the fact that an attacker could potentially exploit this vulnerability to perform unauthorized actions. This could include accessing sensitive information, modifying data, or even gaining control over the system.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nThe best way to mitigate this vulnerability is to update the `http-proxy-middleware` package to a later version where the vulnerability has been resolved. Regularly updating all packages and dependencies to their latest versions is a good practice to prevent potential security issues.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nTo fix this specific vulnerability, you need to update the `http-proxy-middleware` package. Here is how you can do it:\n\n```bash\nnpm uninstall http-proxy-middleware\nnpm install http-proxy-middleware@latest\n```\n\nThis will uninstall the current version of `http-proxy-middleware` and then install the latest version.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `http-proxy-middleware` package requires the following dependencies to execute properly:\n\n- `@types/http-proxy`: \"^1.17.8\"\n- `http-proxy`: \"^1.18.1\"\n- `is-glob`: \"^4.0.1\"\n- `is-plain-obj`: \"^3.0.0\"\n- `micromatch`: \"^4.0.2\"\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)](https://cwe.mitre.org/)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are valid and active at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [],
                "severity": "HIGH",
                "kind": "FAIL"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "d922f28e-809d-3e27-a4ce-63e239f2d092",
              "name": "\"Potential XSS Vulnerability Detected through `ref` Usage\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when an application includes untrusted data in a new web page without proper validation or escaping, or updates an existing web page with user-supplied data using a browser API that can create HTML or JavaScript. XSS allows attackers to execute scripts in the victim's browser which can hijack user sessions, deface web sites, or redirect the user to malicious sites.\n\nIn the context of React, using `React.createRef()` can potentially lead to XSS vulnerabilities if the `ref` is improperly handled. This is because `ref` provides a way to access the properties of a DOM node directly, and if an attacker can control the value of `ref`, they can potentially inject malicious scripts.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should avoid using user-controlled input within your `ref` attributes. If this is not possible, you should properly sanitize and validate all user input to ensure it does not contain any malicious scripts.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `React.createRef()`, consider using callback refs. Callback refs give more fine-grain control over the values, which can help prevent potential XSS attacks.\n\n```javascript\n// Before\nnotificationSystem = React.createRef();\n\n// After\nnotificationSystem = (element) => {\n  this.notificationSystem = element;\n};\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `react`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS (Cross Site Scripting) Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP DOM based XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/DOM_based_XSS_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
