            {
              "id": "dbcc9527-7f20-3c8f-95b6-76722f7a908d",
              "name": "Uncontrolled User Data in <Redirect /> Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Uncontrolled User Data in Redirect Vulnerability\" is a common vulnerability in web applications, including those written in JavaScript. This vulnerability occurs when an application uses input from a user to construct a URL without properly validating or sanitizing the input. This can allow an attacker to construct a URL that redirects a user to an arbitrary location, potentially leading to phishing attacks, session theft, or forcing the user to perform actions without their consent.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the application is redirecting the user to a URL that includes the username of the authenticated user. If the username is not properly validated or sanitized, an attacker could potentially manipulate the URL to redirect the user to an unintended location.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to avoid using user input to construct URLs whenever possible. If it is necessary to include user input in a URL, the input should be properly validated and sanitized to ensure it does not contain any malicious content. This can be done by using a whitelist of allowed characters, encoding special characters, or using a library or function that is designed to safely construct URLs.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn the provided code snippet, you could mitigate the vulnerability by validating and sanitizing the username before using it to construct the URL. Here is an example of how you could do this using a simple whitelist validation:\n\n```javascript\nlet username = auth.getUser().username;\nif (/^[a-zA-Z0-9]+$/.test(username)) {\n  return <Redirect to={`/profile/${username}`}/>\n} else {\n  // Handle invalid username\n}\n```\n\nIn this example, the regular expression `^[a-zA-Z0-9]+$` is used to ensure the username only contains alphanumeric characters. If the username contains any other characters, it is considered invalid and the redirect is not performed.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet appears to be using the following libraries:\n\n- React (for the `<Redirect>` component)\n- Some form of authentication library (for the `auth.getUser()` function)\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Unvalidated Redirects and Forwards Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Unvalidated_Redirects_and_Forwards_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-601: URL Redirection to Untrusted Site ('Open Redirect')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/601.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-601",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/601.html"
