              "id": "f5183680-7af4-3a2b-9142-a7a85e6e620a",
              "name": "\"High Severity Vulnerability in Semver Package, Resolved in Newer Versions\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe Semver package version 6.3.0 has a high severity vulnerability. Semver is a package that provides a programmatic and command-line interface to implement semantic versioning. It is widely used in many Node.js applications and libraries for version comparison, range matching, and version incrementing.\n\nThe vulnerability in this version of Semver can lead to security issues such as arbitrary code execution, denial of service, or information disclosure. This is a significant security concern as it can allow an attacker to execute malicious code, disrupt the service, or gain unauthorized access to sensitive information.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nThe recommended mitigation strategy is to update the Semver package to a newer version that has resolved the vulnerability. Regularly updating your dependencies is a good practice to prevent potential security vulnerabilities.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nTo fix this vulnerability, you need to update the Semver package to the latest version. You can do this by running the following command in your project directory:\n\n```bash\nnpm update semver\n```\n\nAfter running this command, your `package.json` file should be updated with the latest version of Semver.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example provided does not have any explicit dependencies. However, the Semver package itself has the following dependencies:\n\n- lru-cache\n- node-gyp\n- npmlog\n- read-package-json\n- tar\n- which\n\n## OWASP and CWE Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top 10](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [CWE for Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are valid and active for anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [],
                "severity": "HIGH",
                "kind": "FAIL"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "d82654e6-ca19-3aa9-a138-80ac05bce131",
              "name": "\"High-Risk Vulnerability in Semver Package, Resolved in Versions 7.5.2, 6.3.1, 5.7.2 - CVE-2022-25883\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability CVE-2022-25883 is a high-risk vulnerability found in the Semver package used in Docker programming language. This vulnerability is a security concern because it allows an attacker to execute arbitrary code remotely on the affected system. This can lead to unauthorized access, data theft, and potential system damage.\n\nThe specific vulnerability sink is in the `semver` package version `5.7.1` used by `jsonwebtoken` as shown in the provided code snippet. This version of `semver` is known to be affected by the vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to update the `semver` package to a version that has resolved the vulnerability. The vulnerability is resolved in versions `7.5.2`, `6.3.1`, and `5.7.2` of the `semver` package. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nTo fix the vulnerability in the source code, update the `semver` package to a secure version. This can be done by updating the `package.json` file in your project to specify a secure version of `semver`. \n\n```json\n\"dependencies\": {\n  \"jsonwebtoken\": {\n    \"version\": \"your-current-version\",\n    \"semver\": \">=7.5.2\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThen, run `npm install` to update the package.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example provided requires the following library dependencies to execute properly:\n\n- `jsonwebtoken`\n- `semver`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)](https://cwe.mitre.org/)\n- [CVE-2022-25883](https://cve.mitre.org/cgi-bin/cvename.cgi?name=CVE-2022-25883)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [],
                "severity": "HIGH",
                "kind": "FAIL"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "6a929687-62cb-3cff-85a6-697f31e95f3d",
              "name": "\"Avoid Spreading JSX Attributes to Prevent Unintentional Prop Passing and Invalid HTML Warnings\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn JavaScript, particularly when using libraries like React, developers often use the spread operator (`...`) to pass props to components. This can lead to unintentional prop passing and invalid HTML warnings. This is because the spread operator will pass all props, including those that are not explicitly defined or expected by the component. This can lead to unexpected behavior, and in some cases, security vulnerabilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this issue, it is recommended to explicitly pass only the props that are required by the component. This will prevent any unintended props from being passed and will also make the code more readable and maintainable.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the provided code:\n\n```jsx\nreturn (\n    <Route\n    render={props => {\n        if(!auth.getUser() || auth.getUser().role !== 'ADMIN') return <Redirect to={{\n            pathname: '/not-found',\n            state: { from: props.location }\n        }} />;\n        return Component ? <Component location={props.location} /> : render(props);\n    }} />\n);\n```\n\nIn this fixed version, only the `location` prop is explicitly passed to the `Component`.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example provided requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `react`\n- `react-router-dom`\n\n## OWASP and CWE Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top 10](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [CWE for Unrestricted Upload of File with Dangerous Type](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/434.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are valid and active for anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "5e8b8c54-682a-3bd7-848b-bc99c8ec70e5",
              "name": "High-Risk Dockerfile Vulnerability: Lack of Non-Root User Specification",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"High-Risk Dockerfile Vulnerability: Lack of Non-Root User Specification\" is a security vulnerability in Docker where the Dockerfile does not specify a non-root user for running the container. This means that the Docker container runs with root privileges by default. If an attacker manages to break out of the container, they would have root access to the host system, leading to a serious security breach.\n\n## Security Concern\n\nRunning a Docker container as root is a significant security concern because it gives the container unrestricted access to the host system. If an attacker manages to exploit a vulnerability in the container, they could potentially gain root access to the host system, allowing them to execute arbitrary commands, access sensitive data, or disrupt system operations.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to always run Docker containers as a non-root user. This can be achieved by adding a non-root user in the Dockerfile and switching to this user before executing any commands.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the Dockerfile:\n\n```Dockerfile\nFROM node:18.17.1-alpine3.18\n\n# Create a non-root user\nRUN adduser -D nonrootuser\n\n# Switch to the non-root user\nUSER nonrootuser\n```\n\nIn this Dockerfile, the `adduser -D nonrootuser` command creates a new user named `nonrootuser`, and the `USER nonrootuser` command switches to this user. Any subsequent commands in the Dockerfile will be run as this user, not as root.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example does not have any library dependencies as it is a Dockerfile. It only requires Docker to build and run.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-250: Execution with Unnecessary Privileges](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/250.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-250",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/250.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "HIGH",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-250"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "da6baa30-9557-38ae-baf4-78b8ca898c37",
              "name": "Anchor Tag Missing 'noreferrer' Vulnerability: Reverse Tabnabbing Risk",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Anchor Tag Missing 'noreferrer' Vulnerability: Reverse Tabnabbing Risk\" is a security vulnerability in JavaScript that occurs when a link opens a new tab or window, but does not include the 'noreferrer' attribute in the anchor tag. This can potentially allow the new page to access the window object of the original page via the window.opener property, leading to phishing attacks known as \"Reverse Tabnabbing\".\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to always include the 'noreferrer' attribute in the anchor tag when using target=\"_blank\". This will prevent the new page from being able to access the window object of the original page.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe source code can be fixed by adding the 'rel' attribute with the value 'noreferrer' to the anchor tag. Here is the fixed code:\n\n```jsx\n<SocialIcon bgColor=\"#4d5252\" fgColor=\"#ebf2f2\" className=\"ml-1\" style={{ height: 35, width: 35 }} url=\"https://www.linkedin.com/in/borna-nematzadeh/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"/>\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies to execute properly:\n\n- react-social-icons\n\n## OWASP and CWE Resources\n\n- [OWASP HTML5 Security Cheat Sheet - Tabnabbing](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/HTML5_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html#tabnabbing)\n- [CWE-1021: Improper Restriction of Rendered UI Layers or Frames](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/1021.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-1021",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/1021.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-1021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "5ba3de69-6fbc-3990-99dc-4febb2dc1a30",
              "name": "Anchor tag with 'target=\"_blank\"' lacks 'noreferrer', exposing it to reverse tabnabbing vulnerability.",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Anchor Tag Missing 'noreferrer' Vulnerability: Reverse Tabnabbing Risk\" is a security vulnerability in JavaScript that occurs when a link opens in a new tab or window without the `rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"` attribute. This can allow the new page to access the `window.opener` property of the original page, potentially leading to phishing attacks.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always include the `rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"` attribute in anchor tags that open in a new tab or window. This prevents the new page from being able to access the `window.opener` property of the original page.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is how you can fix the vulnerability in the provided code:\n\n```jsx\n<SocialIcon bgColor=\"#4d5252\" fgColor=\"#ebf2f2\" style={{ height: 35, width: 35 }} url=\"https://twitter.com/LogicalHunter\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"/>\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example provided requires the `react-social-icons` library to execute properly.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Resources\n\n- [OWASP HTML5 Security Cheat Sheet - Tabnabbing](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/HTML5_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html#tabnabbing)\n- [CWE-1021: Improper Restriction of Rendered UI Layers or Frames](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/1021.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-1021",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/1021.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-1021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "c4e15dbc-6cbd-3b93-b5c2-af99b489714b",
              "name": "\"Potential Reverse Tabnabbing Vulnerability due to Absence of 'Noopener' in Anchor Tag with Target='_Blank'\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Potential Reverse Tabnabbing Vulnerability due to Absence of 'Noopener' in Anchor Tag with Target='_Blank'\" is a security vulnerability that can occur in JavaScript programming language. This vulnerability arises when a new page is opened through a link with `target=\"_blank\"` attribute, without the `rel=\"noopener\"` attribute. The newly opened page can potentially gain control over the original page through the `window.opener` object, leading to phishing attacks or other security risks.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to always use `rel=\"noopener\"` attribute along with `target=\"_blank\"` in anchor tags. This prevents the new page from being able to access the `window.opener` property and thus safeguards against potential malicious scripts.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe source code fix for the provided code snippet would be:\n\n```jsx\n<SocialIcon bgColor=\"#4d5252\" fgColor=\"#ebf2f2\" className=\"ml-1\" style={{ height: 35, width: 35 }} url=\"https://github.com/bnematzadeh\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"/>\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example provided seems to be using the `react-social-icons` library. Therefore, the library dependency required by the code example to execute properly is:\n\n- react-social-icons\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP HTML5 Security Cheat Sheet - Tabnabbing](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/HTML5_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html#tabnabbing)\n- [CWE-1021: Improper Restriction of Rendered UI Layers or Frames](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/1021.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-1021",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/1021.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-1021"
                ]
              }
            }
